---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Chris Dabel" person>
  <div class="head">
    <img class="headshot" src="/headshot.webp" alt="Chris Dabel" width="600" height="600" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
    <h1>Chris Dabel</h1>
  </div>
  <h2 class="role">Founding data engineer and architect.</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    I build the platform, wire AI into it, and scale it.
  </p>

  <p>
    I've spent twelve years building data pipelines, warehouses, and the
    infrastructure behind production analytics and ML. Most recently the
    founding data engineer at a fintech startup, where I owned the whole
    analytics path solo: ingestion, a Snowflake medallion architecture, star
    schemas, orchestration and data ops in Airflow and Airbyte on AWS, and
    the governance that let an LLM product see only what each client was
    entitled to. Along the way I took the platform off two SaaS vendors, dbt
    Cloud to Airflow on MWAA provisioned in Terraform, and Fivetran to
    self-hosted Airbyte, without a data outage. The same platform also fed
    the model: a labeled corpus in the millions of rows, a harness for
    scoring candidate models against each other, and per-LLM-call cost
    analytics for the AI agents.
  </p>

  <p>
    I use AI as a force multiplier: agentic development with Claude Code and
    Cursor, custom skills and MCP servers, and RAG on Snowflake Cortex. I'm
    experienced enough to know where AI belongs in a system and where it
    doesn't. This site was built that way, and the <a href="https://github.com/cdabel/chrisdabeldotcom">source</a> is public.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sound data governance is key. In regulated industries I've built <em>the principle of least privilege</em>, tenant isolation enforced in
    the database, and credential hygiene into the data platforms I've
    delivered: row access policies over an entitlements table, masking on
    free text and financial values, service accounts on the proper user type
    with rotated keys, and SCIM access-provisioning work alongside the
    security team. It's not glamorous, but it's the prerequisite before I let
    an AI product's systems touch sensitive data at all. I also keep the stack
    patched: dependencies, databases, the orchestrator, and its images. For
    example, I've kept key Python packages updated to stay ahead of
    deprecations, and moved my Python stored procedures to the latest runtime
    Snowflake supports.
  </p>

  <p>
    I do my best work at the edge of a problem nobody has mapped yet. My
    ability to hyperfocus is the engine. I write and build apps to keep the
    creative juices flowing. The pen is my key to innovation. I love to
    inhabit ambiguity, materialize structure inside it, and cut out the fluff
    to ship useful and innovative stuff.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before data, I was an analyst: a State Department internship in Kinshasa,
    working in French, then running a news desk producing global daily news
    summaries for mission-critical operations in government and defense.
    Translating between worlds and empowering decision-making at scale are
    the oldest skills I have. They are what bridge the business question to
    the right technical answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm looking for my next opportunity: founding or staff-level data work in
    high-ambiguity, high-autonomy roles, side by side with leadership that
    values clear, competent communication and architectural decision-making
    to shape collaborative, cross-functional teams.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="elsewhere">
    <h2 id="elsewhere">Elsewhere</h2>
    <ul class="elsewhere">
      <li>
        <a href="/cv/">CV</a>
        <span>What I have built, with the one or two numbers that matter.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/cdabel" rel="me">GitHub</a>
        <span>Including the <a href="https://github.com/cdabel/chrisdabeldotcom">source for this site</a>.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/finally/">Finally</a>
        <span>
          The fintech data platform, built solo in fourteen months. Warehouse,
          orchestration, lender reporting, and the evaluation layer for the
          LLM systems. A case study.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/critpages/">CritPages</a>
        <span>
          The critique app for unpublished writing, in beta. Built solo with
          Claude Code: Expo on the phones, Next.js on Vercel for the web,
          Supabase underneath with the access model in Postgres row-level
          security. <a href="https://critpages.com">critpages.com</a>
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.enternyxus.com/" rel="me">Writing</a>
        <span>Fiction, poetry, and essays as Cael Lawenu, on Substack.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://raisingandconnecting.com">Raising &amp; Connecting</a>
        <span>
          Books, printables, and a blog on emotional intelligence and
          neurodivergence for kids and teens.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/chrisdabel" rel="me">LinkedIn</a>
        <span>The longer version, and the recommendations.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .head h1 { margin: 0; }
  .headshot { width: 8.5rem; height: 8.5rem; border-radius: 12px; flex: none; }
  @media (max-width: 480px) { .headshot { width: 6rem; height: 6rem; border-radius: 10px; } .head { gap: 1rem; } }
  .role { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.4; margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  .elsewhere { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .elsewhere li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0 1rem;
    padding: 0.8rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  @media (min-width: 600px) {
    .elsewhere li { grid-template-columns: 7rem 1fr; }
  }
  .elsewhere > li > a { font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; }
  .elsewhere > li > a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .elsewhere span { color: var(--muted); }
</style>
